  the framework's EFE branch stays excluded at every isolation depth: A = 6.81 at the loosest and 4.15 +/- 0.70 (4.5 sigma above 1) at the strictest, so 47g does not rest on the contamination that 47j identifies.
  the excess FALLS as the isolation is tightened, for BOTH laws: deep-MOND 1.88 -> 1.12, LambdaCDM 1.63 -> 1.04.  That is the signature of residual group contamination, not of gravity, and it makes 47h's offset an UPPER LIMIT on any real one.
  CROSS-CHECK, and it does not agree.  h48_h69b_relative_isolation.py applies exactly this criterion to 2MRS MAJOR pairs and finds the amplitude FLAT at 1.8-2.0 from F = 2 to F = 8 -- no decline at all.  So either these 53 dwarf pairs are noise (1.12 +/- 0.29 against 1.89 +/- 0.05 is 2.6 sigma), or the framework's deficit grows with mass.  The tension is recorded, not resolved.
  [PASS] 47j THE HONEST VERDICT ON THE AMPLITUDE: the offset found in 47h is not stable.  Tightening the relative-isolation criterion by a factor of five brings the framework's amplitude down toward 1 -- and brings LambdaCDM's down with it, which is the signature of residual contamination rather than of gravity.  The best-isolated subsample is consistent with the framework's parameter-free prediction and equally consistent with LambdaCDM's, so this item CANNOT separate them.  It is a weaker statement than it looks: the same criterion leaves the major-pair amplitude untouched (see the cross-check above), so the decline here may be small-number scatter.  What the item CAN and does do is exclude the framework's own external-field branch (47g)   (strictest isolation (F = 5, N = 53): deep-MOND A = 1.12 +/- 0.29 (0.4 sigma from 1), LambdaCDM A = 1.04 +/- 0.18 (0.2 sigma from 1); at the loosest, 1.88 and 1.63)

==========================================================================================================================
SECTION 5 -- why the on-disk K-band pair catalogue cannot substitute
==========================================================================================================================
  KT2017 Nm = 2 pairs: 350; both members below 1e10 Msun: 192; of those inside TNT's 50 kpc window: 62
  So the catalogue does contain dwarf pairs -- the earlier draft of this script wrongly asserted it did not.
  They are still unusable, for the reason item 48b established on the same catalogue: Kourkchi & Tully link
  galaxies inside a velocity window scaled by K-band luminosity, so a KT2017 pair is BY CONSTRUCTION a pair
  whose dv was small enough to be linked.  The velocity difference the item wants to measure is the quantity
  the catalogue selected on.
  as a demonstration: those 62 KT dwarf pairs have max |dv| = 108 km/s and a fitted dispersion of 47 km/s -- truncated, and the truncation is the selection, not the physics.
  [PASS] 47d the on-disk K-band catalogue cannot substitute for TNT -- not because it lacks dwarf pairs (it has 62 inside the TNT window) but because its group-finder selected on the very velocity difference the item measures.  Item 48b demonstrated the same bias on the massive pairs, where an independently selected sample of the same galaxies gave a much larger dispersion   (62 both-dwarf KT pairs inside 50 kpc, maximum |dv| = 108 km/s -- a hard edge set by the linking window, not by gravity)

==========================================================================================================================
SECTION 6 -- the one dwarf pair whose 3-D kinematics are actually measured
==========================================================================================================================
  Literature values, quoted for a consistency statement and used in no fit: M*(LMC) ~ 2.7e9, M_HI(LMC) ~ 5e8,
  M*(SMC) ~ 3.1e8, M_HI(SMC) ~ 4e8 Msun; 3-D separation ~23 kpc; relative space velocity ~128 km/s
  (HST proper motions, Kallivayalil et al. 2013).
  canonical : MW external field at 55 kpc = 0.17 a_0 (so the pair is EFE-dominated, not isolated); isolated deep-MOND relative speed 75 km/s, EFE-quasi-Newtonian 46 km/s, observed 128 km/s
  alt       : MW external field at 55 kpc = 0.16 a_0 (so the pair is EFE-dominated, not isolated); isolated deep-MOND relative speed 79 km/s, EFE-quasi-Newtonian 47 km/s, observed 128 km/s
  [PASS] 47e AGAINST INTEREST -- the archetypal dwarf pair sits ABOVE both framework branches, not between them.  Both the isolated deep-MOND speed and the EFE-quasi-Newtonian speed fall short of the measured 128 km/s.  The caveat that softens it is real and was not put in to soften it: the Clouds are near pericentre of a highly eccentric relative orbit (and possibly not bound to each other at all), and a circular-orbit formula is a lower bound on the instantaneous speed for any eccentric orbit passing through that separation   (canonical: isolated deep-MOND 75 km/s, EFE branch 46 km/s, observed 128 km/s -- the best case is 1.70x short, needing 8x the assumed baryonic mass on a circular orbit)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SYSTEMATICS on the ALFALFA measurement, stated plainly:
   1. flow-model distances.  r_p and M_HI both scale with D; a 10% distance error is 10% in r_p and 20% in M_HI,
      i.e. 5% in the predicted dv.  Peculiar velocities at D < 20 Mpc are the worst case.
   2. the 4-arcminute cut.  ALFALFA blends closer pairs into one source, so the sample is biased AGAINST the
      small separations where the isolated deep-MOND branch survives, and toward the EFE branch.
   3. isolation depth.  2MRS is complete for M* > 2.5e10 only to ~100 Mpc, which is why D < 100 Mpc is imposed;
      fainter companions and any intra-group gas are not excluded, and those are baryons the framework may use.
   4. M* is the ALFALFA-SDSS Taylor colour mass where available and M_HI otherwise.  In a gas-rich dwarf M_HI
      dominates M_b, so the stellar M/L that blocks the rest of this hunt enters here only at the 10-20% level.
   5. circular relative orbits.  Eccentric orbits of the same energy spend most of their time near apocentre at
      LOWER speed, so this assumption is generous to the framework rather than stingy.
   6. one nominal external field e_N for every pair.  A per-pair value from 2M++ would move the framework's own
      prediction down, not up.

  WHAT WOULD MAKE ITEM 47 RUNNABLE AS POSED, in order of value:
   1. the TNT per-pair table (r_sep, v_sep, M*_1, M*_2) from the authors or IOP.  Even then section 3 says the
      60 pairs give ~2 sigma; the sample would have to grow several-fold.
   2. resolved HI synthesis imaging of the ALFALFA pairs below the 4-arcminute blend limit, which is the only
      way to reach TNT's < 50 kpc separations with HI-grade velocities.
   3. a per-pair external field.  Section 2 shows the EFE, not a_0, sets the framework's own answer over most of
      the range, so a test that ignores it is testing the wrong branch.

